From: AR0234 port <local>
Subject: [PATCH 0012] vin: fix the power reference-count hole in __vin_pipeline_close()

`close(open("/dev/video0"))` with no ioctl in between - which is what any
device-enumerating tool (v4l2-ctl --all, the udev helper v4l_id, a desktop
probe) does - prints three lines of noise into dmesg on this SoC:

  sunxi-vin-core 5830000.vinc: Runtime PM usage count underflow!
  WARNING: CPU: 5 PID 5059 at drivers/regulator/core.c:3012 _regulator_disable
  sunxi:vin:[ERR]: vin_pin_disable: disable vind_mclkpin error, fail to disable!

One missing reference count produces all three.  __vin_pipeline_open() does
three things:

	if (... mdev)
		vin_video_core_s_power(p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE], 1);   /* pm_runtime_get_sync */
	vin_md_set_power(vind, 1);                                   /* use_count++ */
	vin_pin_enable(vind);                                        /* regulator_enable x3 */

and __vin_pipeline_close() undoes them, but only the middle one is reference
counted: vin_md_set_power() returns early when vind->use_count is already 0.
vin_pin_disable() and vin_video_core_s_power() are unconditional, so a close
without a partner open releases the mclk pin regulator and the capture node's
runtime PM one extra time.  The regulator core answers with a WARN and -EIO
(that is the stack trace), pm_runtime_put_sync answers with the underflow.

Measured on the Orange Pi Zero 3W (2026-09-16): five open()+close() pairs on
/dev/video0 give five rounds of exactly those three messages, and zero of them
after this patch.  Functional impact is nil (no frame loss, no panic) but the
noise buries the boot-time evidence in the 1900-line ring buffer.

Signed-off-by: AR0234 port <local>
---

--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
@@ -1292,15 +1292,36 @@
 
 	vind = entity_to_vin_mdev(&sd->entity);
 	if (vind) {
+		/*
+		 * Only undo what __vin_pipeline_open() actually did.
+		 *
+		 * The three operations below look symmetric with the open path, but
+		 * only vin_md_set_power() is reference counted (vind->use_count);
+		 * vin_pin_disable() and vin_video_core_s_power() are unconditional.
+		 * vin_open() itself does not touch the pipeline - the pipeline is
+		 * only opened by the first S_FMT/S_INPUT - so open()+close() with no
+		 * ioctl in between reaches here with use_count == 0 and each of the
+		 * two unordered operations fires one extra time:
+		 *
+		 *   sunxi-vin-core 5830000.vinc: Runtime PM usage count underflow!
+		 *   WARNING: CPU: 5 PID ... at drivers/regulator/core.c _regulator_disable
+		 *   vin_pin_disable: disable vind_mclkpin error, fail to disable regulator!
+		 *
+		 * vin_md_set_power(vind, 0) is itself a no-op at use_count == 0, which
+		 * is exactly what exposes the other two.  Put all three behind the
+		 * same reference count so a close without a partner open is a no-op.
+		 */
+		if (vind->use_count) {
 #ifdef CSIC_SDRAM_DFS
-		csic_chfreq_disable(vind->id);
-		vin_chfreq_clk_set(0);
+			csic_chfreq_disable(vind->id);
+			vin_chfreq_clk_set(0);
 #endif
-		vin_md_set_power(vind, 0);
-		vin_pin_disable(vind);
-		if (p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE] && p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE]->entity.graph_obj.mdev)
-			/* power off the ppu */
-			vin_video_core_s_power(p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE], 0);
+			vin_md_set_power(vind, 0);
+			vin_pin_disable(vind);
+			if (p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE] && p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE]->entity.graph_obj.mdev)
+				/* power off the ppu */
+				vin_video_core_s_power(p->sd[VIN_IND_CAPTURE], 0);
+		}
 	}
 
 	__vin_save_sensor_info(vind);
